MP3 decoder fixtures for tests/t_mp3dec.nim
===========================================

Each <name>.mp3 is a short clip (0.4 to 2 s) and <name>.ref is what
upstream's decoder (FreeNetRadio's vendored nanomp3-core, built with
default-features = false, Decoder::new(), i16 output) does with it. The
format of a reference is described at the top of tests/t_mp3dec.nim.

The clips are synthetic: sweeps, tones, clicks and noise bursts made with
ffmpeg 9.1 and encoded with LAME 4.0, Layer II with
ffmpeg's mp2 encoder. Names give the MPEG version (m1, m2, m25), the sample
rate, the channel mode (mono, s = stereo, js = joint stereo, ms = forced
mid/side) and the bit rate.

  m1_44k_js128_id3            ID3v2 tag in front
  m1_44k_js128_junk           2500 bytes of junk in front, false headers in it
  m1_44k_js128_midstart       starts inside a frame a third of the way in
                              (bit reservoir unavailable at first)
  m1_44k_js128_damaged        flipped bits in side info and main data, an
                              impossible big_values, a lost header
  m1_44k_js128_crc            CRC-protected frames
  m1_44k_s192_truncated       ends inside a frame
  m1_44k_free200              free-format bit rate
  m1_44k_vbr_v3, m2_22k_vbr_v6   VBR, 8 to 320 kbit/s
  m1_44k_mono_stereo_switch   mono, stereo, mono, stereo in one stream
  *_intensity*                intensity stereo
  *_mixed*                    mixed blocks
  m1_44k_mono_modeext64       mono frames with mode-extension bits set
  layer2_44k_192              Layer II (reported, not decoded)
  the rest                    plain CBR at the rates the names give; all the
                              clips have short and start/stop blocks

No encoder at hand produces intensity stereo or mixed blocks, so those are
switched on in the headers and side info of streams encoded without them
(regen/mp3tool.py patch). What they decode to is noise, but it goes through
the real decoding paths and upstream agrees on every sample.

noref/ holds two 8 kHz MPEG-2.5 streams with mixed blocks. No encoder makes
those either, and upstream cannot decode them: its mixed-block table for that
rate disagrees with its long/short split and the decoder panics with an
index out of bounds. The port stops reordering at the granule's end instead;
the test only checks that these decode without a fault.

Regenerating
------------

  sh regen/mkfix.sh <outdir>          # the clips (byte-identical with the
                                      # tool versions above)
  # the references: build regen/mp3ref.rs as a binary crate that depends on
  #   nanomp3-core = { path = "<FreeNetRadio>/vendor/nanomp3-core",
  #                    default-features = false }
  # (cargo build --offline --release), then for every clip outside noref/
  { echo "# window 0"; mp3ref <clip> 0; echo "# window 2000"; mp3ref <clip> 2000; } > <name>.ref

`t_mp3dec trace <clip> <window>` prints the port's trace in the same format.
